# HARNESSES.unreached — runnable harnesses that NO Makefile recipe invokes.
#
# Enforced by `make harness-census` (scripts/harness-census.sh, #8302). Every
# runnable harness under test/incus/ and scripts/userspace-*.sh must either be
# invoked by a Makefile recipe — directly or transitively through another
# harness that is itself invoked — or be listed here with a one-line reason.
#
# Format: <repo-relative path><whitespace><one-line reason>
# A line with no reason is a FAIL: a declaration without a reason is a
# suppression, and the whole point of this file is to make someone say, once,
# what the project intends to do with each of these.
#
# THIS LIST IS ONLY ALLOWED TO SHRINK. The census FAILS if an entry here
# becomes reached (delete the line), if an entry does not exist (delete the
# line), or if an entry is not a runnable harness. It cannot be used to
# re-hide a harness later.
#
# Reason vocabulary, so the two cases stay distinguishable:
#   GATE          — asserts a property and can fail; it is owed a Makefile
#                   recipe. Every one of these is a gate the tree built,
#                   unit-tested, documented, and then ran zero times.
#   DIAGNOSTIC    — an operator tool with no verdict; hand-invoked by design.
#   COMPOSED      — only ever run as a step of another harness in this list;
#                   it becomes reached for free when its caller does.
#
# 28 entries on the day this landed (23 under test/incus/, 5 under scripts/).
# 15 of them are GATE.

# ── GATE — built, tested, documented, invoked by nothing ──
test/incus/newflow-ceiling-harness.sh        GATE: #4800 connection-rate ceiling; docs/userspace-newflow-ceiling.md opens "the code ships; the measurement is OWED". Makefile names it only under `bash -n` — lint-reachable, run-unreachable. #9053 ATTEMPTED it and found the block is NOT the wiring: the committed pool rule matches 10.0.61.240/28 while the LAN host is 10.0.61.102 and newflow-gen has no client source-address option, and the pool holds 1000 ports against a 5000-80000 sweep. Both need a change to the SHARED cluster config. See the "Attempted and BLOCKED" section of that doc before re-attempting.
test/incus/test-mouse-latency-matrix.sh      GATE: the full 12-cell #905 mouse-latency matrix; mouse_latency_aggregate.py returns PASS/FAIL/INSUFFICIENT-DATA and nothing calls the harness that feeds it.
test/incus/test-mouse-latency.sh             GATE: one #905 mouse-latency rep. Makefile:524 names it only in a COMMENT ("Run it after touching test-mouse-latency.sh"); the target beside that comment runs the hermetic selftest.
test/incus/test-mouse-latency-same-class.sh  GATE: same-class 1 Gbps variant of the #905 rep — the control for the cross-class default.
test/incus/test-fbf-steering.sh              GATE: #1827 two-upstream FBF steering smoke. `make test-fbf-steering-lib` runs fbf-steering-selftest.sh, not this — registered via a DIFFERENT TARGET's name.
test/incus/cc-rollback-functional.sh         GATE: #1922 commit-confirmed auto-revert must re-apply to the DATAPLANE; a store-only revert is exactly the bug and only a live run can see it.
test/incus/wg-interop.sh                     GATE: #1736 WireGuard interop against an INDEPENDENT kernel-WireGuard peer.
test/incus/reverse-key-collision-probe.sh    GATE: #1760 W2 live-fire NAT reverse-key collision watch.
test/incus/cos-simul-load-smoke.sh           GATE: #1614 all-11-class simul-load acceptance gates. Calls cos-gate1-small-four-alone.sh, and the pair is collectively unreached.
test/incus/cos-gate1-small-four-alone.sh     GATE: #1630 Gate 1 solo >=95% shape check. Only caller is cos-simul-load-smoke.sh, itself unreached.
test/incus/cos-be-contention-harness.sh      GATE: exact-queue behaviour under best-effort contention; docs/cos-best-effort-contention-harness.md.
test/incus/fairness-cos-throughput-headroom.sh GATE: strict-exact vs surplus-sharing headroom comparison; calls fairness-cos-class-sweep.sh.
test/incus/fairness-cos-class-sweep.sh       GATE: per-class multi-sample fairness gap contract, non-zero on any class failing. Only caller is fairness-cos-throughput-headroom.sh, itself unreached.

# ── COMPOSED — reached for free once the caller above gets a recipe ──
test/incus/fairness-harness.sh               COMPOSED: the per-class run driver invoked by fairness-cos-class-sweep.sh; docs/fairness-regimes.md.
test/incus/apply-cos-config.sh               COMPOSED: re-applies the CoS fixture a deploy wipes; invoked by test-mouse-latency.sh, step1-capture.sh, cos-be-contention-harness.sh and fairness-cos-throughput-headroom.sh — all unreached. Also named in a with-cluster.sh COMMENT, which is not an invocation.
test/incus/step1-capture.sh                  COMPOSED: #816 per-cell capture driver, invoked by step1-baseline-and-matrix.sh and step2-sched-switch-capture.sh.

# ── DIAGNOSTIC — operator tools with no verdict; hand-invoked by design ──
test/incus/analyze-userspace-traces.sh       DIAGNOSTIC: reduces xpfd journal worker-stat lines; reports, does not assert.
test/incus/capture-cold-stall.sh             DIAGNOSTIC: #1782 one-shot evidence capture for an overnight idle window.
test/incus/step1-baseline-and-matrix.sh      DIAGNOSTIC: #816 evidence-collection orchestrator; writes into docs/pr/816-step1-rerun/evidence/.
test/incus/step1-classify.sh                 DIAGNOSTIC: post-hoc classifier over a step1 evidence directory.
test/incus/step2-sched-switch-capture.sh     DIAGNOSTIC: #821 sched_switch perf capture composed on step1-capture.sh.

# ── DIAGNOSTIC — the #8341 / #8447 persistent-NAT bisect harnesses ──
#
# Six one-shot probes written to answer ONE question each during the bisect that
# split #8447 out of #8341. Each mutates the shared loss cluster's NAT
# configuration, drives traffic, reads a comparative pair of numbers and prints
# which reading it supports; none asserts a re-runnable invariant, which is what
# would make it a GATE. They are listed rather than given recipes because a
# `make` target implies "run this to check the tree is healthy", and running one
# of these repoints a live cluster's source-NAT rule at a test pool.
#
# #8447 has since been localised — a chassis cluster plus a source rule pointing
# at a `persistent-nat` pool sets ForwardingSupported=false, which disarms
# forwarding before the snapshot publish (the #1449 admission boundary). The
# property that outlives the bisect is bound by Go cells in
# pkg/dataplane/userspace/pnat_disarm_visibility_8447_test.go, which need no
# cluster. These stay as the evidence trail.
test/incus/pnat-catchall-outage-check-8341.sh  DIAGNOSTIC: #8341 severity triage — does retargeting the CATCH-ALL source-NAT rule to a pool break only the probe's flow or ALL transit? Three probes under one committed config; reports a comparison, asserts nothing.
test/incus/pnat-mint-observability-8341.sh     DIAGNOSTIC: #8341 — is a pool-mode persistent-NAT binding never MINTED, or minted and RECLAIMED before the query lands? Distinguishes three states from timed observations.
test/incus/pnat-counter-read-8447.sh           DIAGNOSTIC: #8447 — reads the persistent-NAT admission counter pair to separate "refused at admission" from "installed then reclaimed". Declares VOID when the counters are absent or the fixture is not what it claims.
test/incus/pnat-rule-shape-8447.sh             DIAGNOSTIC: #8447 — controls for RULE SHAPE by running the same A/B behind a dedicated `from`-matched rule instead of the retargeted catch-all.
test/incus/pnat-upstream-stage-8447.sh         DIAGNOSTIC: #8447 — diffs every counter the node publishes across both arms to localise which stage drops the flow. NOTE: it prints only NON-ZERO deltas and does not assert its metric snapshot is non-empty, so an empty scrape is indistinguishable from "every counter flat"; pnat-wedge-recovery-8447.sh is the run that carries the ABSENT sentinel.
test/incus/pnat-wedge-recovery-8447.sh         DIAGNOSTIC: #8447 — is the stop a wedge or config-reversible, and does the apply say anything? Four phases with a time control (phase 2b) and a per-phase log census; `rx_now()` prints ABSENT rather than a number when the series is missing.
scripts/userspace-ha-validation.sh           DIAGNOSTIC: long-form userspace HA validation walkthrough; superseded as a gate by the 8 Makefile HA smokes.
scripts/userspace-ha-failover-validation.sh  DIAGNOSTIC: long-form failover validation walkthrough; superseded as a gate by `make test-failover`.
scripts/userspace-native-gre-validation.sh   DIAGNOSTIC: native-GRE validation walkthrough.
scripts/userspace-perf-compare.sh            DIAGNOSTIC: ad-hoc perf A/B between two builds.
scripts/userspace-phase-cycle.sh             DIAGNOSTIC: wrapper that re-runs userspace-ha-validation.sh across phases.

test/incus/screen-probe.sh	DIAGNOSTIC — #8336 crafted-frame screen probe; arms a screen profile on a LIVE cluster, sends crafted frames and reads the per-reason drop counters, so it needs the shared cluster lock and has no hermetic recipe. Its analysis layer is reached: `make test-screen-probe-lib` drives screen-probe-selftest.sh over synthetic transcripts, which is where the three-valued DROPPED/PASSED/VOID verdict and the #6440 marker gate are bound.
